{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And the priest shall take holy water in an earthen vessel, and of the dust that is on the floor.</b> Why with water, which is from the heavens, and with dust, which is from the earth? They said in tractate Sotah: Rava said, Why did the Torah say to bring dust for the sotah? If she merited, there goes forth from her a son like Abraham our father, of whom it is written (Genesis 18:27): ״ואנכי עפר ואפר״; if she did not merit, she returns to her dust — thus far." "<b>In an earthen vessel.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: Rabbi Meir would say, From where do we learn that with the measure a person measures, it is measured to him? As it is said (Isaiah 27:8): ״בסאסאה בשלחה תריבנה״ — measure for measure (se'ah be-se'ah). And Rabbi Ḥiyya taught: From the day the Temple was destroyed, even though the Great Sanhedrin was abolished and the four court-imposed death penalties were abolished, the judgment of the four death penalties was not abolished: one who was liable to stoning — either he falls from the roof or a beast mauls him; one who was liable to burning — either he falls into a fire or a snake bites him; one who was liable to decapitation — either he is delivered to the kingdom or brigands come upon him; one who was liable to strangling — either he drowns in a river or dies of askara (croup). And likewise with the sotah: with the measure she measured, it is measured to her. She gave him fine wine in fine goblets — therefore the priest gives her the bitter waters in an earthen vessel. She stood at the doorway of her house to see him — therefore the priest stations her at the Nicanor Gate and displays her disgrace to all. She spread a fine kerchief over her head to adorn herself — therefore ״ופרע את ראש האשה״. She adorned her face for him — therefore her face turns green. She painted her eyes for him — therefore her eyes bulge. She fed him the delicacies of the world — therefore her offering is animal feed. She acted in secret — therefore the Place (HaMakom) publicizes her openly. This is what is written (Proverbs 26:26): ״תכסה שנאה במשאון תגלה רעתו בקהל״." "<b>And if</b> you understand well what was said about an earthen vessel, you will find in it an allusion that she will not merit to rise with him at the resurrection of the dead, but will be like a broken earthen vessel that has no repair, if she committed adultery she perishes from this world and the world to come, for one who commits adultery under her husband is like one who serves two authorities. Therefore it says 'an earthen vessel' and did not say 'a glass vessel,' for glass has repair through melting and blowing, and the dead are destined to live again — all the more so than glass. And this is the secret of what they interpreted: 'Amen from this man — this is Amen from another man,' that is to say: Amen from this man who was her mate in soul and in body; Amen from another man, from whom she came — that her soul shall be cut off from their union and she shall not be with either one of them. And understand this."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {1 "סרונכי rendered as 'askara (croup),' the standard Talmudic identification of this death condition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:55.940300516Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}